Elizabeth "Jake" Feinler was born in Wheeling, West Virginia, in 1931, and was the first in her family to go to college. She was working toward a PhD in biochemistry when she took a job indexing chemical compounds. The challenge of organizing huge amounts of information hooked her, and she never went back.

## Running the ARPANET's help desk

In 1972 Doug Engelbart recruited her to his Augmentation Research Center at the Stanford Research Institute. By 1974 she was running the Network Information Center (NIC) for the ARPANET, the network that grew into the internet.

The NIC was the network's information desk. Her team answered users' questions, published directories of people and services, distributed the Request for Comments (RFC) documents that define internet standards, and kept the master list of computer names on the network.

## Naming the internet

- **1974:** Her team and the Network Working Group defined the format for the host table, the list that matched computer names to addresses.
- **1980s:** Her group created the WHOIS directory and the top-level domain scheme that gave us .com, .edu, .gov and .org.
- **1989:** Left the NIC after 17 years as its director.
- **2012:** Inducted into the Internet Hall of Fame.

Without domain names, people would have to type number addresses to reach every site. Her work made the internet usable for everyone else.

